maria: Heads up — tiny repro: our sample app using the JS SDK on the edge runtime is causing weird TS overload/generic inference failures. anyone else hit this?
jon: Hmm, can you paste the error + minimal snippet?
maria: sure — here's the error and a tiny repro

```
src/app.ts:10:8 - error TS2769: No overload matches this call.
  Overload 1 of 2, '(route: string, opts?: { input?: string | undefined; }): Promise<string>', gave the following error.
    Argument of type '{ input: string; }' is not assignable to parameter of type 'string'.

// repro
import Redwood from "redwood-sdk";
const client = new Redwood.Client();

async function run() {
  const res = await client.generate<{ userId: string }>("summarize", { input: "hi" });
  // expected res to be { userId: string }
}
```

kevin: That looks like an ESM/CJS interop plus typing mismatch — sometimes default vs named export resolution causes TS to pick the wrong overload. Are you bundling for edge with esbuild/rollup?
sophia: Related: we've seen tsconfig module/moduleResolution matter here. If project is using `module: "esnext"` (node target) TS can resolve different signature variants vs `module: "nodenext"`. Small repro in older projects.
maria: the sample app we ship uses a pretty conservative tsconfig because customers copy it — we can't force nodenext on all users. Want to avoid recommending breaking changes.
devx-bot: FYI docs: <https://internal.devx.redwood/docs/edge-ts-interop> — short note about moduleResolution + bundles.
sam: Two quick possibilities: 1) overloads + generics sometimes lose inference when the function is imported via a CommonJS-compatible shim. Try using an explicit generic param at call site as a workaround: `client.generate<{ userId: string }>("summarize", { input: "hi" } as any)` or 2) change the SDK typing to expose a single generic signature instead of overloads.
kevin: Another nuance: esbuild/rollup can rewrite `exports`/`module` entry points and TS sees `.d.ts` from the wrong file. We publish dual packages — make sure the `types` field in package.json points to the ESM types entry (`dist/esm/index.d.ts`) and that `typesVersions` maps node->nodenext if needed.
sophia: For the SDK itself we could add a non-overloaded fallback signature like `generate<T = any>(route: string, opts?: unknown): Promise<T>` so consumers on funky tsconfigs still get inference. Not ideal but pragmatic.
maria: poking at the SDK, quick local hack that fixes our repro:

```
// in sdk types
export function generate<T = any>(route: string, opts?: { input?: string }): Promise<T>;
// (single generic signature, no overloads)
```

jon: If we change SDK types, add a changelog note. Also can recommend `skipLibCheck: true` in sample tsconfig as a temporary mitigation (reduces friction, but hides real type mismatches).
sophia: We should add a tsd/tsd-lite test that compiles the edge bundle under `module: "esnext"` and `module: "nodenext"` to catch this. I can add that to the SDK repo tests.
kevin: Also check whether our published `package.json` `exports` map includes a dedicated `./edge` entry that points to an ESM-bundled build + correct d.ts. That often fixes runtime + typing divergence for edge consumers.
maria: Great — I'll open an issue with a minimal repro repo and tag eng-runtime + devx. Will attach the failing sample and the small workaround patch.
jon: sgtm. If you want I can draft the issue template for eng-runtime with build artifacts to speed triage.
sam: tiny tidbit — sometimes `unknown` vs `any` matters here: making the param `opts?: unknown` forces callers to assert and can avoid accidental inference collapse, but it's a breaking change. Use cautiously.
maria: noted. thanks everyone — will post link when I open the issue. :thumbsup:
